  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 357 AM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nAn upper low remains stationary over northern CA early this morning \nwhile a ridge holds over the southeastern US and is starting to \nretreat back west. Mid-level moisture is noted streaming north \nthrough the meridional flow over the Four Corners region. At the \nsurface, a warm front has a slight southwest-northeast orientation \nfrom roughly Minneapolis through Hiawatha. Dense fog has been \nobserved north of that boundary via satellite, observations, and \nwebcams, and expect that to remain the case through mid to late \nmorning as soundings show the fog mixing out around 15-16z. Areas \njust to the south of the front may still see some patchy fog given \nlight winds, clear skies, and residual low-level moisture from \nrecent rainfall. A few light showers from isentropic ascent have \ndeveloped across part of north central KS, mainly the far northwest \ncorner of the CWA, which should move into Nebraska further into the \nmorning as the warm front lifts north. This leaves us with another \nwarm day in store as highs reach the 90s for most. Locations north \nof I-70 may stay in the mid to upper 80s with early fog lifting to \nstratus before that eventually moves north as well, leaving less \ntime for insolation.\n\nHeading into Friday, the upper ridge continues to retrograde over \nthe Southern Plains while the low starts to move east ever so \nslowly. A lead shortwave ejecting out of the High Plains ahead of \nthat system results in a deepening sfc low in western KS. An early \nlook at short-term guidance indicates that the associated cold front \nprobably won't make it far enough east for precip to develop in our \nnorth central KS counties during the late afternoon or early \nevening. There is a low chance for storms that develop ahead of the \nfront in southern Nebraska to graze northern KS overnight, which \ncould bring strong winds if they make it into our area. The highest \nrain chances are focused on the frontal passage Saturday. There are \nstill varying ideas on the speed of the front with the NAM being the \nfastest solution, GFS being on the slower side, and Euro somewhere \nin between. The location of the front by late afternoon will then \ndetermine where thunderstorms have the best chance of initiating. In \nany case, modest shear and instability would support a few \nmarginally severe storms along the front. As long as the front is \nprogressive enough, that should limit flooding concerns. However, it \nshould be noted that Pwat values around 1.6-2\", while not nearly as \nhigh as this last rain event, are still on the high end of \nclimatology so would support efficient rainfall and could result in \nhigher QPF if the system moves slower. The upper low looks to fill \nas it moves out of the Rockies and across the Northern Plains the \nrest of the weekend. PVA from that system keeps rain chances around \nthrough Sunday before dry conditions return Monday.\n\nCAA behind the front as well as clouds and rain bring cooler \ntemperatures starting Sunday with highs in the 70s and even a few \nupper 60s. The cooler air mass remains in place through mid-week, \nwhich would be more fall-like if it wasn't for the dew points \nstaying in the 60s and 70s. The pattern looks to stay fairly active \nwith low-amplitude ridging keeping dry conditions in place through \nTuesday, followed by another shortwave bringing slight chances for \nrain Wednesday.",
